            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Against the products in our <a href="/best-concrete-countertop-sealer/">best concrete countertop sealer</a> guide, Tuff Duck is the cheap, forgiving end of the range. A water-based polyurethane such as the guide's top pick builds a tougher film that holds up better at the sink, at the cost of a fussier application. A penetrating sealer keeps the raw look but offers little against acid. An epoxy gives the deepest gloss but scratches and can mark with heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            So who should buy it? Someone sealing a bathroom vanity, a fireplace hearth, a desk or a first countertop who values an easy job and accepts recoating as routine. Who should skip it? Anyone with a busy family kitchen, a lot of citrus and wine, and no appetite for touch-ups. If your countertops are natural stone rather than concrete, none of this applies. Marble wants an impregnating sealer, which we cover in our <a href="/stonetech-bulletproof-sealer-review/">StoneTech BulletProof review</a>.
          </p>
