        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it for a busy kitchen and expecting decades</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tuff Duck is a thin acrylic. The critical owner reports we found describe wear at the sink and in the main prep area, in one case within about a year. If you cook daily with acidic ingredients, a polyurethane film former will need far less attention. Buy Tuff Duck for its ease, not for longevity.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating "food safe" as a certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The listing calls the formula food safe and non-toxic, but names no standard behind the claim. Treat the cured surface as fine for incidental contact and keep cutting and direct food prep on a board. Never judge it food safe before the film has fully cured.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying it like paint with a brush</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Owners and testers who used a brush saw brush marks; those who used a microfiber pad or fine roller got an even film. Thin coats in alternating directions work better than one heavy coat, which can stay soft, cloud or streak as it dries.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over wax, old sealer or damp concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A film-forming acrylic needs clean, bare, fully dry concrete to bond. Wax and old sealer stop it adhering, and moisture in the slab clouds it. Strip and clean first, wait out the 28-day cure on new work, and test on a sample before the real top.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the water test after it cures</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thin films vary in thickness, and thirsty patches of concrete take more product. A few drops of water beside the sink and in the prep zone will show any spot that absorbs. Another thin coat there now is far easier than a stain later.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes Tuff Duck concrete countertop sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rocklinite Labs LLC. Its website is titled "Makers of Tuff Duck," and the Amazon product information lists Rocklinite Labs as the manufacturer. It is not a Concrete Countertop Solutions product, despite occasional claims online. Rocklinite also sells a separate Tuff Duck granite, grout and marble sealer, which is a different formula and should not be used in its place.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Tuff Duck a penetrating sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Partly. The listing says it penetrates the concrete while leaving a durable shell on the surface, and gives the material as acrylic. In practice it behaves as a thin-film acrylic: it beads water and adds a satin to semi-gloss sheen, and it wears in the busiest spots first. It is neither a pure penetrant nor a thick polyurethane.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Tuff Duck sealer food safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The seller describes the water-based formula as food safe and non-toxic, but we found no named certification or published safety data sheet behind that. Treat the fully cured surface as suitable for incidental food contact, use a cutting board for prep, and choose a sealer with documented food-contact compliance if you need one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of Tuff Duck do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the instruction sheet in the box. Owners commonly describe two to three thin coats, applied with a microfiber pad or fine roller in alternating directions and allowed to dry between coats. A very absorbent slab may take more. Do a water test after curing and add a thin coat only where water still darkens the concrete.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does Tuff Duck take to cure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We could not confirm an official figure; published accounts range from 24 to 48 hours before light use. The film keeps hardening for several days after it feels dry, so keep water, heat and acids off it for the first week, and use the time given on the insert in your box.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do Cheng sealer reviews say compared with Tuff Duck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are water-based acrylic thin-film sealers, so reviews describe similar strengths: easy application, a natural sheen and good water beading, with recoating needed over time. The bigger difference today is availability. Cheng's store is no longer online and several retailers list its sealer as unavailable, so check stock before planning around it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should Tuff Duck be reapplied?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no published interval we could verify. One owner reported wear within about a year, while a DIY blogger planned on reapplying every two to three years. Let the surface tell you. When water stops beading beside the sink or in the prep area, clean the area and add a thin coat. Busy kitchens will need this sooner than vanities.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

